{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.95,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": [
        "R2"
      ],
      "pins": [
        "R2.1",
        "R2.2",
        "D3.1",
        "U1.31"
      ],
      "nets": [
        "Net-(D3-A)",
        "+3V3"
      ],
      "observed": "R2 is shown as a 10 Ω pull-up on the FAN_1 tach path: R2 pin 2 is on +3V3 and R2 pin 1 is on Net-(D3-A), which also connects to D3 pin 1 and U1 pin 31 (GPIO21). Its LCSC field is C84376, the same LCSC code used for R5/R6/R11, which are 10 kΩ. The SPICE deck also models R2 as 10 Ω.",
      "expected": "The fan-tach pull-up should be a high-value pull-up like the counterpart R11 on the FAN_0 path, which is 10 kΩ. A 10 Ω pull-up is not a normal fan-tach input pull-up and would force excessive current when the tach line is pulled low or when the GPIO is driven low.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(comp (ref \"R2\")\n      (value \"10\")"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(comp (ref \"R11\")\n      (value \"10k\")"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (code \"15\") (name \"Net-(D3-A)\")"
        },
        {
          "source": "spice",
          "quote": "R2 Net-_D3-A_ +3V3 10"
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "pull-up/pull-down resistors R2/R5/R6/R11 (strapping/EN/fan-tach related"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "When FAN_1_TACH is pulled low, the 10 Ω pull-up can draw roughly 330 mA from +3V3, overheating R2 and loading the fan tach output. It can also damage or falsely trip the GPIO21 input path, making the FAN_1 tachometer behave very differently from the FAN_0 path with R11 = 10 kΩ.",
      "confidence": 0.95
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Most connectivity and major component choices match the specification, but R2 is a 10 Ω pull-up in a fan-tach input where the symmetric part R11 and the LCSC code indicate a 10 kΩ pull-up, which is a functional design anomaly."
}

